feat 1.0

# SPEC-ADPKUR-004 — Kurrent adapter: the TLS server name on Node 26 (an amendment to SPEC-ADPKUR-001, in a spec of its own). Agreed by founder ruling 2026-11-10, chosen verbatim: "Flip it (Recommended)",
# with the open choices accepted as "Exact loopback only, New code TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED, nodeMajor seam, File the grpc-js issue"
# (the issue is grpc/grpc-node#3131; the rule can go when grpc-js stops sending an IP as the servername). Earlier ruling,
# chosen verbatim: "A: loopback→localhost (Recommended)". DESIGN.md beside this file holds the rule's rationale and the open choices.
# Integration tier only: this file is excluded from the spec tier (specs.exclude) like transport.feat and broker-loss.feat.
#
# The fault: on Node 26, Node refuses an IP address as the TLS server name (ERR_INVALID_ARG_VALUE, "Setting the TLS ServerName to an IP
# address is not permitted"). @grpc/grpc-js 1.14.7 sets connectionOptions.servername from the remote host unconditionally, and
# @kurrent/kurrentdb-client 1.3.2 exposes no channel option to stop it, so a `kurrentdb://` connection string with `tls=true` (the
# client's default when `tls` is absent) and an IP host cannot connect. `tlsVerifyCert=false` does not help: the name is still set.
#
# The rule (the adapter's instrument connection, SPEC-ADPKUR-001; the system's own client is the system's concern):
#   1. A host that is exactly the IPv4 loopback address 127.0.0.1 or the IPv6 loopback address ::1 is connected to as `localhost`. Only the
#      host changes: credentials, port, path and every parameter are as the user wrote them.
#   2. Any other IP address host (an IPv4 or IPv6 literal, 127.0.0.2 included) with tls=true, when the running Node's major version is 26 or
#      later, is refused at setup() with TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED, before the adapter connects or appends anything.
#   3. A hostname, tls=false, or a non-loopback IP host on a Node below 26 is left exactly as it is.
# The rewrite of rule 1 applies on every Node major, not only 26, so the loopback path is one path on the whole CI matrix and is
# observable on Node 20, 22 and 24. Rule 2 reads the Node major through `options.nodeMajor` (an integer, default the running Node's
# `process.versions.node` major), the seam by which a scenario puts the adapter on Node 24 or 26 without a second Node.
#
# Driven through the probe of SPEC-ADPKUR-001 (adapters/kurrent/fixtures/probe.mjs, inside touches, not shipped).
# `ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host, tls?, nodeMajor?, script? }` builds the connection string
#   kurrentdb://admin:<password>@<host>:<port>?tls=<tls>&tlsVerifyCert=false     (tls default true; a v6 host is written in brackets)
# with the fixture node's port (the test's private secure dev node of SPEC-ADPKUR-001) and the admin identity, creates the adapter for
# namespace `a` with `options.nodeMajor` set when `nodeMajor` is given, and runs `script` (default `setup:a`; setup() runs only where the script says so,
# so a scenario that never needs the node never connects). The script
# uses the steps of SPEC-ADPKUR-001 plus two that never touch the network:
#   target:<ns>   the host the adapter hands the client, as the client sees it: `target:a=localhost`
#   params:<ns>   the connection string the adapter hands the client with the secret, the host and the port removed:
#                 `params:a=admin?tls=true&tlsVerifyCert=false` (user name, then the query string exactly as given)
# Both read an adapter method `connectionTarget()` that returns the string the client is created from; the probe redacts it.
# A refused setup is a rejection, so nothing else is traced.
# The fixture hosts: 127.0.0.1 and `[::1]` reach the fixture node (its port is published on both loopbacks; TLS names are not verified,
# tlsVerifyCert=false); 192.0.2.10 and 2001:db8::1 are documentation addresses nothing listens on, which is the point of a refusal that
# happens before any connection. The connecting scenarios need the fixture node.

spec SPEC-ADPKUR-004 "Kurrent adapter: a loopback IP host is connected to as localhost and any other IP host is refused on Node 26"
context Toolchain
aggregate Adapters
type infrastructure
status agreed

construct:
  handler at adapters/kurrent/src/index.ts
  touches adapters/kurrent/**
  touches feat.config.json
  needs SPEC-ADPKUR-001
  Parse the connection string's hosts with the WHATWG URL parser (the adapter already does for `options.system`), so `[::1]` and `[0:0:0:0:0:0:0:1]` are both ::1.
  Define a loopback host as exactly 127.0.0.1 or ::1. When the connection string has tls=true (the default when `tls` is absent) and
  a host is an IP literal: if it is a loopback host, replace that host, and only the host, by `localhost` in the string the client is created from;
  otherwise, when `options.nodeMajor` (default: the major of `process.versions.node`) is 26 or more, refuse setup() with
  TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED before the first connection. A connection string with several hosts is judged host by host, in order, and
  the first refused host is the one the detail names. A hostname, a `kurrentdb+discover://` string, or tls=false is passed through unchanged.
  Add `connectionTarget()` to the adapter: the string the client is created from, with the credentials of `options.system` applied;
  it does no network and never throws for a refusal (setup() throws). Add `nodeMajor` to the adapter's options (integer, 1 or more).
  The detail of TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED names the host and the cause: the IP literal, "tls=true", "Node 26" (the running major) and the remedy
  (a hostname the certificate names, or tls=false). It carries no credential.

enforce:
  rejects TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED when the host is an IP literal other than 127.0.0.1 and ::1, tls is true and the Node major is 26 or more, before any connection
  a host that is exactly 127.0.0.1 or ::1 with tls=true is connected to as localhost, and only the host changes
  a hostname, tls=false, and a non-loopback IP host on a Node below 26 are connected to exactly as the user wrote them
  the rewrite is in the string the client is created from only: the captured records, the keys, the namespace and the connection string the user gave are unchanged

contract:
  input ObserveInput
  response ObserveResult
  error ObserveError

scenario "an IPv4 loopback host is connected to as localhost, and the node is reached":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "127.0.0.1", script: "setup:a target:a start:a write:a:orders:Placed settle:a stop:a" }
  predict success:
    response OK ObserveResult { trace: "setup:a target:a=localhost start:a write:a settle:a=true stop:a=1", last: "orders/Placed" }
    filesystem has []

scenario "an IPv6 loopback host is connected to as localhost, and the node is reached":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "[::1]", script: "setup:a target:a start:a write:a:orders:Placed settle:a stop:a" }
  predict success:
    response OK ObserveResult { trace: "setup:a target:a=localhost start:a write:a settle:a=true stop:a=1", last: "orders/Placed" }
    filesystem has []

scenario "the loopback rewrite holds on Node 26, where the IP name would be refused":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "127.0.0.1", nodeMajor: 26, script: "setup:a target:a start:a write:a:orders:Placed settle:a stop:a" }
  predict success:
    response OK ObserveResult { trace: "setup:a target:a=localhost start:a write:a settle:a=true stop:a=1", last: "orders/Placed" }
    filesystem has []

scenario "the rewrite changes the host and nothing else":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "127.0.0.1", script: "params:a" }
  predict success:
    response OK ObserveResult { trace: "params:a=admin?tls=true&tlsVerifyCert=false" }
    filesystem has []

scenario "a hostname is connected to as written, on Node 26 too":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "localhost", nodeMajor: 26, script: "setup:a target:a start:a write:a:orders:Placed settle:a stop:a" }
  predict success:
    response OK ObserveResult { trace: "setup:a target:a=localhost start:a write:a settle:a=true stop:a=1", last: "orders/Placed" }
    filesystem has []

scenario "the rewrite is invisible in what is captured, so a record is the same through an IP host and through localhost":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "127.0.0.1", script: "setup:a start:a write:a:orders:Placed write:a:billing:Charged settle:a stop:a read:a" }
  predict success:
    response OK ObserveResult {
      trace: "setup:a start:a write:a write:a settle:a=true stop:a=2 read:a=2",
      last: "orders/Placed,billing/Charged"
    }
    filesystem has []

scenario outline "an IP host other than the two loopback addresses is refused on Node 26 before the adapter connects to anything":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: <host>, nodeMajor: 26, script: "setup:a" }
  predict rejection TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED:
    response ERR ObserveError { code: "TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED", detail: matching <detail> }
    filesystem has []
  examples:
    | host           | detail         |
    | "192.0.2.10"   | "192.0.2.10"   |
    | "[2001:db8::1]" | "2001:db8::1" |
    | "127.0.0.2"    | "127.0.0.2"    |

scenario "the refusal names the cause and the remedy":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "192.0.2.10", nodeMajor: 26, script: "setup:a" }
  predict rejection TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED:
    response ERR ObserveError { code: "TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED", detail: matching "tls=true.*Node 26.*hostname.*tls=false" }
    filesystem has []

scenario "a later Node major is refused the same way":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: "192.0.2.10", nodeMajor: 28, script: "setup:a" }
  predict rejection TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED:
    response ERR ObserveError { code: "TLS_HOST_UNSUPPORTED", detail: matching "Node 28" }
    filesystem has []

scenario outline "an IP host is left as written where the name is allowed, so no refusal and no rewrite":
  when: ObserveKurrentTlsHost { host: <host>, tls: <tls>, nodeMajor: <nodeMajor>, script: "target:a" }
  predict success:
    response OK ObserveResult { trace: <trace> }
    filesystem has []
  examples:
    | host           | tls   | nodeMajor | trace                          |
    | "192.0.2.10"   | false | 26        | "target:a=192.0.2.10"  |
    | "192.0.2.10"   | true  | 24        | "target:a=192.0.2.10"  |
    | "127.0.0.1"    | false | 26        | "target:a=127.0.0.1"   |
